{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/38256-carving-out-your-own-far-shores","id":38256,"title":"Carving out your own Far Shores","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-28T05:37:12-08:00","id":38256,"title":"Carving out your own Far Shores","content":"One thing I wish we had was methods for how the Far Shores were carved out. What do wraiths use to build their own heaven's and hells? I mean we have one new Arcanos in Swar that is used there, and that is a good example. And we have Moliate. But I think it would have been nice to get a Far Shores book, and it would of been a great chance to toss in some powers, both I guess in the form of Arcanoi and in the form of potent Artifacts/Relics. Or maybe even some new type of power for manipulating the Underworld.<br /> <br /> One thing though I think was always cool was the concept of non-Wraith magic carving out some of these places. We get that with beings like Nhudriel, and Mage and Mummy Magics. I like the idea of more spirits sometimes sneaking into the Underworld. There used to be a pretty cool article I read a long time ago suggesting that Potent Chimeras in the Dreaming may die and become a type of Malfean. And I like the notion that perhaps some of the Far Shores may not be completely in the Underworld, perhaps some are in halfway places between the Underworld and other Realms of existence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-28T10:31:26-08:00","id":38333,"title":"","content":"Hello Eldagusto,<br /> <br /> I'm with you, a book on the Far Shores would have been great. As for their construction, the shining ones that rule them are more or less on par with a ferrymen when it comes to rough power level. My assumption was that they simply used the allure of their particular afterlife to gather sufficient followers to build their realms. Moliate and Soul Forging can go a long way. Would love to see new examples of artifacts/tempered arcanoi and or/ancient/new arts of the far shores.<br /> <br /> In a pinch, I find &quot;The Book of the Dead&quot; from the NWOD to be helpful when it comes to far shore ideas/construction. I really like the idea of &quot;old laws&quot; being in place that are enforced by the realms guardians (which could be akin to something like the shining ones or their minions). Its not a perfect fit, but I think its close enough to offer a range of interesting places for the players to go.<br /> <br /> Pax,<br /> <br /> Dale","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;The Mercy of Order is Justice&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1391-aeoris","name":"Aeoris","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-28T12:32:14-08:00","id":38381,"title":"","content":"Indeed, a stand alone book would be good or failing that a chapter on the Far Shores in a big book of the geography of the Underworld.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-28T17:06:29-08:00","id":38498,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">I never did anything with KotE, or the \u201cMiddle Kingdom\u201d in general, but I did borrow from its cosmology. <br /> <br /> In many Year of the Lotus supplements, I got the distinct impression that different worlds (Yang, Yin, and Yomi) had ways of interconnecting to one another. It\u2019s something I always kept in my mind, but never came up in-story. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/38256-carving-out-your-own-far-shores#post38256\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I like the notion that perhaps some of the Far Shores may not be completely in the Underworld, perhaps some are in halfway places between the Underworld and other Realms of existence.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. I always liked the notion that maybe some parts of the Labyrinth (in the Underworld) may connect to Malfeas (from the Werewolf mythos), or that there might be a Far Shore that connects with parts of the Umbra. Like I said, it never came up in my troupe\u2019s stories, but \u2013 if the opportunity presented itself \u2013 I would have liked to incorporate those \u201ccross-worlds\u201d elements. </span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-28T20:31:04-08:00","id":38563,"title":"","content":"My own take on it was that the Far Shores existed previously akin to atolls in the Tempest, eventually &quot;settled&quot; by the Shining Ones, and once Byways were established, they &quot;trucked in&quot; any necessary items. I've always thought that the Far Shores were gradually warped and molded by their inhabitants, given the malleable nature of a primarily psychic landscape, and that one could discern the age and relative &quot;power&quot; of a Far Shore by the amount of reconfiguring done to the land.<br /> <br /> A book (or a chapter, or <i>something</i>) would go a long way to helping explain this.<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}